✅ 2.Why do we call start()?

🔴 start() is used to start a new thread of execution.
🔴 When we call start(), it does not directly execute the run() method.
🔴 Instead, it requests the JVM to create a new thread. The JVM then asks the underlying Operating System to create the actual thread.
🔴 Once the thread is created, it is placed in the RUNNABLE state.
🔴 The Thread Scheduler decides when that thread gets CPU time, and only then is the run() method invoked.

Internally Flow :

Main Thread
       │ calls start()
       ▼
Thread.start()
       │
       ▼
JVM invokes native start0() method
       │
       ▼
Operating System creates a new thread
       │
       ▼
Thread state becomes RUNNABLE
       │
       ▼
Thread Scheduler decides when to execute it
       │
       ▼
run() method is invoked










